> Velveting is what gives Chinese restaurant beef that impossibly tender texture. The baking soda raises the pH and relaxes the proteins, while cornstarch creates a protective coating that locks in moisture. Don't skip the rest in the marinade—it's not optional.

== Velvet the Beef ==

In a bowl, combine the @sliced flank steak{450%g} with:
- @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}
- @Shaoxing wine{1%tbsp}
- @cornstarch{1%tsp}
- @baking soda{¼%tsp}
- @neutral oil{1%tbsp}

Mix thoroughly until the beef is fully coated. Set aside for ~{10%minutes}.

== Prepare the Sauce ==

In a small bowl, whisk together:
- @oyster sauce{2%tbsp}
- @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}
- @sugar{1%tsp}
- @broth{60%ml}
- @cornstarch{½%tsp}

Set aside. The cornstarch will settle, so give it a stir before using.

== Sear the Beef ==

Heat @neutral oil{2%tbsp} in a #wok{} over high heat until the wok just begins to smoke.

Add the marinated beef in a single layer—do not stir yet. Sear for ~{1%minute} undisturbed to develop a golden crust. Then stir-fry for another ~{1%minute} until the beef is just browned but not cooked through. Remove and set aside.

== Stir-fry ==

In the same wok, add a touch more @neutral oil{½%tbsp} if needed. Add @minced garlic{2%cloves} and @minced ginger{1%tsp}; cook for ~{15%seconds} until fragrant.

Pour in the mixed sauce and let it bubble and thicken for ~{30%seconds}.

Return the beef to the wok, then add the @fresh bean sprouts{250%g} (patted completely dry). Toss everything rapidly over high heat for exactly ~{60%seconds}—no more, or the bean sprouts will turn soggy and lose their crunch.

Serve immediately, plated alongside jasmine rice.